---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
import { services, fmtDate, humanValue } from '../../lib/data.js';

const svc = services.CA.get('visitor-visa');
const ok = svc.records.filter(r => r.status === 'ok');
const fastest = ok.slice(0, 10);
const slowest = ok.slice(-10).reverse();
const median = Math.round(svc.medianDays);
const big = ['IN', 'NG', 'PH', 'PK', 'CN', 'BR', 'MX', 'BD', 'VN', 'CO']
  .map(cc => ok.find(r => r.applicant_country === cc)).filter(Boolean);
---
<Base title={`Canada visitor visa processing times by country, analyzed (median ${median} days)`}
      description={`Where visitor-visa waits stand right now: fastest and slowest applicant countries, the ${median}-day median, major corridors like India, Nigeria and the Philippines, and how to read the spread.`}>
  <nav class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/guides/">Guides</a> › Visitor visa by country</nav>
  <h1>Canada visitor visa times, analyzed by country</h1>
  <p class="lede">IRCC currently publishes visitor-visa processing times for <strong>{ok.length}</strong> applicant countries (of {svc.records.length} tracked). The median is <strong>{median} days</strong>, but the spread is enormous — the same visa takes {fastest[0].value_raw} from {fastest[0].applicant_country_name} and {slowest[0].value_raw} from {slowest[0].applicant_country_name}. Data as of {fmtDate(svc.latestEffective)}.</p>
  <AdSlot position="guide-top" />

  <h2>Major corridors right now</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Applying from</th><th>Official time</th><th>vs. typical</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {big.map(r => (
        <tr>
          <td><a href={`/canada/visitor-visa/${r.applicantSlug}/`}>{r.applicant_country_name}</a></td>
          <td class="num">{humanValue(r)}</td>
          <td>{r.speed ? <span class={`chip ${r.speed.cls}`}>{r.speed.label}</span> : '—'}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>The ten fastest — and what "fast" hides</h2>
  <div class="related">
    {fastest.map(r => <a href={`/canada/visitor-visa/${r.applicantSlug}/`}>{r.applicant_country_name}: <strong class="num">&nbsp;{r.value_raw}</strong></a>)}
  </div>
  <p>Short published times cluster in countries with modest application volumes and high approval predictability. A {fastest[0].value_raw} figure does not mean the visa office is idle — it often means the recent sample skews toward straightforward cases.</p>

  <h2>The ten slowest</h2>
  <div class="related">
    {slowest.map(r => <a href={`/canada/visitor-visa/${r.applicantSlug}/`}>{r.applicant_country_name}: <strong class="num">&nbsp;{r.value_raw}</strong></a>)}
  </div>
  <p>Long tails usually combine heavy demand, additional screening, and local capacity limits. If your country sits here, the practical advice is unglamorous: apply far earlier than feels necessary, and watch the recorded history on your route's page — sustained improvement over several weeks is a real signal, a single good week is not.</p>

  <h2>Read the movement, not just the level</h2>
  <p>Because IRCC recalculates weekly from completed cases, each route's history (which we record and the official site does not keep) tells you whether a corridor is clearing or clogging. Every country page on this site shows that history alongside today's figure — see <a href="/canada/visitor-visa/">the full table of all {svc.records.length} countries</a>.</p>

  <div class="prov">Computed from the tracked dataset at build time; updates automatically with every refresh. Source: IRCC. Not legal advice.</div>
  <AdSlot position="guide-bottom" />
</Base>
